From: NeoDarwin
Subject: [PATCH] msun: sinpi and cospi parity for |x| >= 2^32 on arm64

sinpi() and cospi() take the parity of the integer part of |x| from
(uint32_t)x, after subtracting 2^30 once. For |x| >= 2^32 + 2^30 the value
is still out of uint32_t's range, which C leaves undefined: x86 keeps the
low bits, but arm64's fcvtzu saturates to 0xffffffff, so every such
argument counts as odd and the result has the wrong sign (sinpi(x) for
x = -0x1.7762b5c12c71dp+47 returns +0.29 instead of -0.29). Converting
through uint64_t, which holds any integer below 2^52 exactly, keeps the low
bits on every architecture. The float versions are unaffected (|x| < 2^23
there). FreeBSD/aarch64 has the same bug; candidate for upstreaming.

Rebase-risk: low (one expression in each of two files).
---
diff --git a/lib/msun/src/s_cospi.c b/lib/msun/src/s_cospi.c
--- a/lib/msun/src/s_cospi.c
+++ b/lib/msun/src/s_cospi.c
@@ -124,7 +124,7 @@
 
 		if (j0 > 30)
 			x -= 0x1p30;
-		j0 = (uint32_t)x;
+		j0 = (uint32_t)(uint64_t)x;	/* NeoDarwin: low bits, not saturation */
 		return (j0 & 1 ? -c : c);
 	}
 
diff --git a/lib/msun/src/s_sinpi.c b/lib/msun/src/s_sinpi.c
--- a/lib/msun/src/s_sinpi.c
+++ b/lib/msun/src/s_sinpi.c
@@ -139,7 +139,7 @@
 
 			if (j0 > 30)
 				x -= 0x1p30;
-			j0 = (uint32_t)x;
+			j0 = (uint32_t)(uint64_t)x;	/* NeoDarwin: low bits, not saturation */
 			if (j0 & 1) s = -s;
 		}
 
